name: hold space
boss: Palimpsest
flavor: Erase me and I remember anyway.
brief: `sed` keeps a second buffer alongside the line it is working on: the
brief: hold space, empty until you touch it. `h` copies pattern space into
brief: it, `H` appends instead of overwriting, `g` copies it back, `G`
brief: appends it back, and `x` swaps the two outright.
brief:
brief: `N` reads one more line into the pattern space instead of starting a
brief: new cycle -- now a pattern can span two lines. `P` prints just the
brief: first of them, `D` deletes it and restarts the cycle without reading
brief: a fresh line, which is how a script walks the file two lines at a
brief: time without ever losing its place.
brief:
brief: `:label` marks a spot, `b label` jumps to it unconditionally, `t label`
brief: jumps to it only if the last `s///` actually changed something --
brief: which is how a substitution loops until nothing is left to fix.
brief:
brief: Addresses pick which lines a command runs on: ``$`` is the last line, ``1`` the first,
brief: and ``!`` inverts the pick -- `$!d` deletes every line except the last, `1!G` runs
brief: `G` on every line but the first. `printf 'a\nb\n' | sed '$!d'` prints ``b``;
brief: `sed '2!d'` keeps only line 2. A `!` before a command means "unless the address
brief: matches", for `b` just the same.
brief:
brief: Lowercase `n` prints the pattern space (unless `-n`), then loads the next line over it (`N`
brief: appends instead). `{ }` groups commands under one address: `sed -n '2{p;p}'`
brief: prints line 2 twice. Inside a pattern, ``\n`` matches the newline in a two-line
brief: pattern space and ``\1`` repeats what group 1 matched, so ``\(x*\)\n\1`` matches
brief: a run of x's, a newline, then the very same run again.
---
setup: tac isn't always around, but sed's hold space can do its job
q: {reverse the line order of @cfg@ using `sed`'s hold space, no `tac`|read @cfg@ backwards with `sed` alone, not `tac`|flip @cfg@ top-to-bottom with `sed`, no `tac` involved}
a: sed '1!G;h;$!d' @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: every line appends itself before whatever G already holds, then h saves it back -- only the last line's turn prints, by when the hold has all of it in reverse
---
setup: you want to skim a dense @cfg@ with breathing room between lines, without editing the file
q: {count @cfg@'s lines once `sed`'s bare `G` double-spaces it, `wc`|count @cfg@'s lines after `sed`'s `G` pads each one, using `wc`|how many lines is @cfg@ after `sed` `G`-pads it, counted with `wc`}
a: sed 'G' @cfg@ | wc -l
out: ^[0-9]*[02468]$
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: G appends the hold space, still empty, after every line -- one blank line per real line, so the total count always comes out even
---
setup: @log@ carries one line straight after a byte-identical copy of itself, and neither d nor s alone can compare a line to its neighbor
q: {`sed` out @log@'s adjacent repeat, `grep` count ``connection refused``|drop @log@'s repeat with `sed`, `grep` count ``connection refused``|`sed` away @log@'s repeated line, `grep` the ``connection refused`` count}
a: sed '$!N; /^\(.*\)\n\1$/!P; D' @log@ | grep -c 'connection refused'
out: ^1$
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: N pulls the next line in beside this one; if the backreference finds them equal nothing prints, otherwise P prints the first and D loops on the second
---
setup: you need @cfg@ as one line to search across a value, and there's no fixed number of lines to N through
q: {join @cfg@ into one space-separated line, with `sed`|flatten @cfg@ to a single line with `sed`, spaces between|collapse @cfg@ onto one line with `sed`, space-joined}
a: sed ':a;N;$!ba;s/\n/ /g' @cfg@
out: listen.*port.*timeout
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: the label and b pull in every remaining line first -- only once N has swallowed the whole file does the final s/// turn the newlines into spaces
---
setup: two settings need to trade places line-for-line through the whole file, not just the first pair
q: {swap every pair of adjacent lines in @cfg@, with `sed`|trade each line of @cfg@ with the one after it, using `sed`|reorder @cfg@ two lines at a time, each pair flipped, with `sed`}
a: sed -n 'h;n;p;x;p' @cfg@
cmp: ref
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: h banks the first line of the pair, n reads the second and p prints it, then x swaps the bank back in for the first -- a leftover odd line has no partner and is dropped
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: printf 'a\nb\n' | sed -n '1h;1!H;${g;s/\n/+/;p}'
a: a+b
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: the first line seeds the hold space with h; every line after that appends with H; nothing prints until the last line copies the whole thing back with g
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: printf 'aaaa\n' | sed ':a;s/aa/a/;ta'
a: a
ref: sed
teach: sed
hint: each pass eats one pair of a's; t loops back to :a only while the last s/// actually matched, so it keeps squeezing until a single a is left
